The command SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'event%'; is used to check the operational status and configuration of the MySQL 
Event Scheduler.
Here is a detailed breakdown of how it works:

Core Breakdown

SHOW VARIABLES: Instructs MySQL to list its system configuration variables and their current values.

LIKE: Filters the results so you do not have to scroll through hundreds of system variables.

'event%': The % symbol is a wildcard. It tells MySQL to return any variable whose name begins with the word "event".

The Output Variable: event_scheduler
When you run this command, it primarily checks the event_scheduler system variable, 
which can have three states:

ON: The event scheduler thread is active and running. 
It will scan for scheduled events (like your delete_retirees event in the image) and execute them on time.

OFF: The scheduler is stopped. Existing events are paused and will not run, 
though you can still create or alter events.DISABLED: The scheduler is completely locked down. 
It cannot be turned on dynamically using SET GLOBAL without restarting the server with a config change.

SET GLOBAL event_scheduler = ON;
SET GLOBAL event_scheduler = OFF;




